                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "57c6ef05-2fa6-352c-bdca-273da6bdd9cf",
                        "entry_id": "4d3866e8-1cd5-322b-bde1-0be2f10ba398",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of an object: derisorily small, tiny; of a person: puny, stunted."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Scotland"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5bc9a7ad-3887-417c-8882-a44e221cfa1f",
                                "sense_id": "57c6ef05-2fa6-352c-bdca-273da6bdd9cf",
                                "text": "Wurlie, 1. Contemptibly puny, or small in size; as \"a wurlie bodie,\" an ill-grown person, Fife, Loth."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e09d8751-1f0a-4af6-9a17-243d66949286",
                                "sense_id": "57c6ef05-2fa6-352c-bdca-273da6bdd9cf",
                                "text": "Thy wee roun' pate sae black and curly, / Thy twa bare feet, sae stoure an' burly, / The biting frost, though snell an' surly / An' sair to bide, / Is scouted by thee, thou hardy wurly, / Wi' sturdy pride."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "85d23036-a83c-48c6-8a86-435ea3bbd88a",
                                "sense_id": "57c6ef05-2fa6-352c-bdca-273da6bdd9cf",
                                "text": "Wurly [wur·li], adj. A very small portion of anything is of a wurly size; gen. 'What a wurly bit o' bread, and nought on 't!'[…], i.e. no butter, or anything on. The r is often strongly trilled in this word."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3e6827ef-d288-4bf2-9e3e-dd2edbc2079f",
                                "sense_id": "57c6ef05-2fa6-352c-bdca-273da6bdd9cf",
                                "text": "WIRL, sb. Sc. Yks. […] A small and harsh-featured person; an ill-grown child; a stunted animal. […] Hence (1) Wirly, adj. puny, small; (2) Wirly-bit, sb. a short time; a little way; a small portion. (1) Sc. There's nae a pilchard in my creel, Nor wurlie sprat … They're firm and fat (Jam.)."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "13149bda-2816-3d22-988e-3a10b9ef8193",
                        "entry_id": "4d3866e8-1cd5-322b-bde1-0be2f10ba398",
                        "glosses": [
                            "gnarled, knotted; wizened, wrinkled."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Scotland"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ceacd8d5-d76b-46e3-bdfb-a6502df3ffd7",
                                "sense_id": "13149bda-2816-3d22-988e-3a10b9ef8193",
                                "text": "Wurlie, […] 2. Rough, knotted; as, \"a wurlie rung,\" a knotted stick, S. It is applied to a stick that is distorted, Lanarks. As this sense, however, is considerably remote from the other, the term may have had a different origin. 3. Wrinkled, applied to a person; as, a wurly body, Lanarks."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
